#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:1
msgid "Feature Name: `mitigation_enforcement`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:2
msgid "Start Date: 2025-09-13"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:3
msgid ""
"RFC PR: [rust-lang/rfcs#3855](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3855)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:4
msgid ""
"Tracking Issue: [rust-lang/rust#154613](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/154613)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:6
msgid "Summary"
msgstr "摘要"

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:9
msgid ""
"Introduce the concept of \"mitigation enforcement\", so that when compiling "
"a crate with mitigations enabled (for example, `-C stack-protector`), a "
"compilation error will happen if the produced artifact would contain Rust "
"code without the same mitigations enabled."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:14
msgid ""
"This in many cases would require use of `-Z build-std`, since the standard "
"library only comes with a single set of enabled mitigations per target."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:17
msgid ""
"Mitigation enforcement should be disableable by the end-user via a compiler "
"flag."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:20
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr "動機"

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:23
msgid ""
"Memory unsafety mitigations are important for reducing the chance that a "
"vulnerability ends up being exploitable."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:26
msgid ""
"While in Rust, memory unsafety is less of a concern than in C, mitigations "
"are still important for several reasons:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:29
msgid ""
"Some mitigations (for example, straight line speculation mitigation, [`-Z "
"harden-sls`](https://github.com/rust-lang/compiler-team/issues/869)) "
"mitigate the impact of Spectre-style speculative execution vulnerabilities, "
"that exist in Rust just as well as C."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:32
msgid ""
"Many Rust programs also contain large C/C++ components, that can have memory "
"vulnerabilities. The exploitation of these vulnerabilities is made easier by "
"the presence of mitigation-less Rust code within the same address-space."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:36
msgid ""
"Many Rust programs use `unsafe`, that can introduce memory unsafety and "
"vulnerabilities."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:39
msgid ""
"Mitigations are generally enabled by passing a flag to the compiler (for "
"example, [`-Z harden-sls`](https://github.com/rust-lang/compiler-team/"
"issues/869) or [`-Z stack-protector`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/114903)). If the compilation process of a program is complex, it is "
"very easy to end up accidentally not passing the flag to one of the "
"constituent object files."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:44
msgid "This can have one of several consequences:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:46
msgid ""
"In some cases (for example `-Z fixed-x18 -Z sanitizer=shadow-call-stack`), "
"the mitigation changes the ABI, and linking together code with different "
"mitigation settings leads to undefined behavior such as crashes even in the "
"absence of an attack. In these cases, the sanitizer should be a [target "
"modifier](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3716) rather than using "
"this RFC."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:51
msgid ""
"For \"Spectre-type\" mitigations (e.g. `harden-sls`), if there is some "
"reachable code in your address space without a retpoline, attackers can "
"execute a Spectre attack, even if there is 0 UB in your code."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:54
msgid ""
"For \"CFI-type\" mitigations (e.g. kcfi), if there is reachable code in your "
"address space that does not have that sanitizer enabled, attackers can use "
"it to leverage an already-existing memory vulnerability into ROP execution, "
"even if the memory vulnerability is in a completely different part of the "
"code than the part that has the mitigation disabled"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:59
msgid ""
"For \"local\" mitigations (e.g. stack protector, or C's `-fwrapv` - which I "
"don't think Rust has), the mitigation protects the code when it is in the "
"right place relative to the bug - a stack protector helps basically when it "
"protects the buffer that overflows, and it does not matter which other "
"functions have a stack protector."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:64
msgid ""
"To avoid these consequences, teams that write software with high security "
"needs - for example, browsers and the Linux kernel - need to have a way to "
"make sure that the programs they produce have the mitigations they want "
"enabled."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:68
msgid ""
"On the other hand, for teams that write software in a more uncoordinated "
"environment, it can be hard to chase down all dependencies, and especially "
"for \"local\" mitigations, being able to enable them on an object-by-object "
"basis is the only thing that allows for the mitigations to actually be "
"deployed. Especially important is progressive deployment - it's much easier "
"to introduce mitigations 1 crate at a time than to introduce mitigations a "
"whole program at a time, even if the end goal is to introduce the "
"mitigations to the entire program."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:81
msgid "Supported mitigations"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:83
msgid "The following mitigations could find this feature interesting"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:85
msgid "Already Stable"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:87
msgid ""
"`-C control-flow-guard` This is a \"CFI-type\" mitigation on Windows, and "
"therefore having it enabled only partially makes it far less protective."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:91
msgid ""
"However, it is already stable, and we would need a `-C deny-partial-"
"mitigations=control-flow-guard` or `-C control-flow-guard-enforce` (or "
"bikeshed) to make it enforcing."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:95
msgid "We could also make it enforcing over an edition boundary."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:96
msgid ""
"`-C relocation-model` If position-dependent code is compiled into a binary, "
"then ASLR will not be able to be enabled."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:100
msgid ""
"This is less of a problem in Rust than in C, since position-independent code "
"is a rarely-changed default in Rust, and it can easily be checked via "
"[`hardening-check(1)`](https://manpages.debian.org/testing/devscripts/"
"hardening-check.1.en.html) or similar tools since it's easily visible in the "
"ELF header."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:105
msgid ""
"However, we might still want to introduce a `-C deny-partial-"
"mitigations=position-independent` or `-C enforce-position-independent`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:108
msgid ""
"As far as I can tell, there is no way to disable `relro` via stable Rust "
"compilation flags."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:110
msgid ""
"`-C overflow-checks` This can be thought of as a mitigation in some sense. "
"It might make sense to add a way to make it enforcing, but I don't think it "
"makes sense to make it enforcing by default since that is contrary to the "
"normal use of turning overflow checks only for the crate under development."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:116
msgid ""
"However, we might still want to introduce a `-C deny-partial-"
"mitigations=overflow-checks` or `-C enforce-overflow-checks`. It probably "
"does not make sense to make it enforcing over an edition boundary, since the "
"desired default there is not to enforce. This probably merits a separate RFC/"
"FCP."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:122
msgid "Currently Unstable (as of rustc 1.89)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:124
msgid ""
"This RFC is not the place to make a decision of exactly which unstable "
"mitigations should have enforcement enabled - that should take place as a "
"part of their stabilization."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:128
msgid ""
"However, it would be good to see that enforcement fits well with sanitizers."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:130
msgid ""
"[`-Z branch-protection`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/113369)/[`-"
"Z cf-protection`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/93754) - control "
"flow protection in ARM or Intel, respectively. Would probably want this. It "
"uses [`.note.gnu.property`](#notegnuproperty-1) which makes it active only "
"if every object in the address space uses it, which makes it easy to detect "
"via a [`hardening-check(1)`](https://manpages.debian.org/testing/devscripts/"
"hardening-check.1.en.html)\\-style tool, but since it is not the default, "
"this would make it easier to make sure it is enabled."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:136
msgid ""
"[`-Z ehcont-guard`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/build/reference/"
"guard-enable-eh-continuation-metadata?view=msvc-170) - Similar for [`-Z "
"branch-protection`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/113369)/[`-Z cf-"
"protection`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/93754), but for "
"Windows exception handlers - if every object in the address space uses it, "
"`NtContinue` is only allowed to jump to valid exception handlers. This means "
"it is easy to detect via a [`hardening-check(1)`](https://manpages.debian."
"org/testing/devscripts/hardening-check.1.en.html)\\-style tool, but it "
"probably makes sense to include it."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:141
msgid ""
"[`-Z indirect-branch-cs-prefix`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/116852) - a part of retpoline (Spectre) mitigation on x86."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:143
msgid ""
"[`-Z no-jump-tables`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/116592) - CFI-"
"type mitigation? soon to be stabilized as [`-C jump-tables`](https://github."
"com/rust-lang/rust/pull/145974). Do we want to hold that stabilization as "
"well?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:146
msgid ""
"[`-Z retpoline`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/116852) and `-Z "
"retpoline-external-thunk` - Spectre-type mitigation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:147
msgid ""
"[`-Z sanitizer`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/89653)\\-based "
"mitigations. A fairly large use case. As far as I can tell, enforcement "
"makes sense for `-Zsanitizer=kcfi, -Zsanitizer=memtag, -Zsanitizer=shadow-"
"call-stack` and does not make sense for `-Zsanitizer=address, -"
"Zsanitizer=dataflow, -Zsanitizer=hwaddress, -Zsanitizer=leak, -"
"Zsanitizer=memory, -Zsanitizer=thread` (`-Z sanitizer=address` should "
"probably be a [target modifier](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3716))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:152
msgid ""
"[`-Z stack-protector`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/114903) - "
"stack smashing protection, local-type mitigation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:153
msgid ""
"[`-Z ub-checks`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/123499): as far as "
"I can tell, it is not intended as a mitigation, but since it prevents some "
"UB, it might be thought of as one"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:167
msgid "Guide-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:170
msgid ""
"When you use a mitigation, such as `-C stack-protector=strong`, if one of "
"your dependencies does not have that mitigation enabled, compilation will "
"fail."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:173
msgid ""
"Error: your program uses the crate `foo`, that is not protected by `-C stack-"
"protector=strong`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:176
msgid ""
"Recompile that crate with the mitigation enabled, or use `-C allow-partial-"
"mitigations=stack-protector` to allow creating an artifact that has the "
"mitigation only partially enabled."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:180
msgid ""
"It is possible to disable `-C allow-partial-mitigations=stack-protector` via "
"`-C deny-partial-mitigations=stack-protector`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:183
msgid ""
"Other flags that can be mitigations, for example `-C overflow-checks=on`, "
"permit partial mitigations by default, but it is possible to make sure your "
"dependencies have the same mitigation setting as you by passing `-C deny-"
"partial-mitigations=overflow-checks`. That flag can be overridden by `-C "
"allow-partial-mitigations=overflow-checks`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:189
msgid "Reference-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:192
msgid ""
"For every mitigation-like option, the compiler CLI flags determines whether "
"that mitigation allows or denies partial mitigations. This can be turned on "
"via `-C allow-partial-mitigations=<mitigation>` and turned off by `-C deny-"
"partial-mitigations=<mitigation>` (for example, `-C allow-partial-"
"mitigations=stack-protector` and `-C deny-partial-mitigations=stack-"
"protector`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:199
msgid ""
"These flags act like every other compiler flag, with the last flag winning "
"if there are multiple values for the same mitigation."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:202
msgid ""
"When a mitigation option appears (for example, `-C stack-protector=strong`), "
"the mitigation is denied for partial use. This is needed to allow for "
"distributors and the like to set a default that allows partial mitigations "
"without weakening the security of users."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:206
msgid "For example,"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:212
msgid ""
"Will allow partial mitigations for stack-protector (since there's an allow) "
"but deny partial mitigations for `overflow-checks` (since the later deny "
"overrides the allow) and `kcfi` (since `-Csanitizer=kcfi` resets enforcement "
"for CFI)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:216
msgid "It is possible to bikeshed the exact naming scheme."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:218
msgid ""
"Every new mitigation would need to decide whether it adopts this scheme, but "
"mitigations are expected to adopt it."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:221
msgid ""
"Every crate gets a metadata field that contains the set of mitigations it "
"has enabled."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:223
msgid ""
"When compiling a crate, if the current crate has a mitigation with "
"enforcement turned on, and one of the dependencies does not have that "
"mitigation turned on (whether enforcing or not), a compilation error results."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:228
msgid ""
"If a mitigation has multiple \"levels\", a stricter level at a dependency is "
"compatible with a looser level at the current (dependent) crate, but not "
"vice-versa - for example, if the standard library crates were compiled with "
"`-C stack-protector=all` (not discussing whether that is a wise idea), they "
"would be compatible with every configuration of user crates."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:234
msgid ""
"The error happens independent of the target crate type (you get an error if "
"you are building an rlib, not just the final executable)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:237
msgid ""
"For example, with `-C stack-protector`, the compatibility table will be as "
"follows:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:240
msgid "Dependency\\Current"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:240
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:242
msgid "none"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:240
msgid "none+allow"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:240
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:244
msgid "strong"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:240
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:245
msgid "strong + allow partial"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:240
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:246
msgid "all"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:242
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:243
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:244
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:245
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:246
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:247
msgid "OK"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:242
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:243
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:244
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:245
msgid "error"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:242
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:243
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:244
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:245
msgid "OK - current allows partial"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:243
msgid "none + allow partial"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:247
msgid "all + allow partial"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:249
msgid "Drawbacks"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:252
msgid "Rationale and alternatives"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:255
msgid "Syntax alternatives"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:257
msgid "`-C my-mitigation-noenforce`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:259
msgid ""
"Instead of `-C allow-partial-mitigations`, it is possible to split every "
"flag value that enables a mitigation for which enforcement is desired is "
"split into 2 separate values, \"enforcing\" and \"non-enforcing\" mode. The "
"enforcing mode is the default, non-enforcing mode is constructed by adding `-"
"noenforce` to the name of the value, for example `-C stack-protector=strong-"
"noenforce` or `-C sanitizer=shadow-call-stack-noenforce`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:265
msgid ""
"If a program has multiple flags of the same kind, the last flag wins, so e."
"g. `-C stack-protector=strong-noenforce -C stack-protector=strong` is the "
"same as `-C stack-protector=strong`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:269
msgid "\\-C stack-protector=none-noenforce"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:271
msgid ""
"The option `-C stack-protector=none-noenforce` is the same as `-C stack-"
"protector=none`. I am not sure whether we should have both, but it feels "
"that orthogonality is in favor of having both."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:275
msgid "Limiting the set of crates that are allowed to bypass enforcement"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:277
msgid ""
"You could have a syntax like `-C allow-partial-mitigations=stack-"
"protector=@stdlib+foo`, `-C stack-protector=strong-noenforce=@stdlib+foo`, "
"or some other syntax (using `+` since `,` should have a different level of "
"precedence), which would only allow the mitigation to be partial on a "
"specified set of crate names."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:282
msgid ""
"`@stdlib` is used here to stand for all the sysroot crates, since the user "
"does not want to specify them all (should we bikeshed the syntax?)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:285
msgid ""
"This is different from `-C pretend-mitigation-enabled` (which would be a "
"mitigation equivalent of `-C unsafe-allow-abi-mismatch`), since it reflects "
"a decision made by the application writer (dependent crate) rather than the "
"library writer."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:289
msgid "This can be done in a later stabilization that the core of the feature."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:291
msgid "Impacts to syntax choices"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:293
msgid ""
"The `-C stack-protector=strong-noenforce=std+alloc+core` syntax feels ugly."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:295
msgid ""
"If we decide that the noenforce syntax is important to allow for flag "
"concatenation, we can certainly have both the `noenforce` syntax and the "
"`allow-partial-mitigations` syntax, with `noenforce` disabling enforcement "
"for all crates while `allow-partial-mitigations` disables it only for "
"specific crates."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:300
msgid "Interaction with `-C unsafe-allow-abi-mismatch`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:302
msgid ""
"The proposed rules do not interact with `-C unsafe-allow-abi-mismatch` at "
"all, so if you have a \"sanitizer runtime\" crate that is compiled with the "
"following options:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:305
msgid ""
"\\-C no-fixed-x18 -C sanitizer=shadow-call-stack=off -C unsafe-allow-abi-"
"mismatch=fixed-x18 -C unsafe-allow-abi-mismatch=shadow-call-stack"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:307
msgid ""
"Then dependencies will need to use it with `-C allow-partial-"
"mitigations=shadow-call-stack` rather than `-C sanitizer=shadow-call-stack`, "
"otherwise they will get an error."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:310
msgid ""
"As far as I can see, there is no current demand for that sort of sanitizer "
"runtime, but if that is desired, it might be a good idea to add a `-C "
"pretend-mitigation-enabled=shadow-call-stack` (which would act like `-C "
"allow-unsafe-api-mismatch` and mark a crate as a \"wildcard\"), and possibly "
"to make `-C unsafe-allow-abi-mismatch` act like `-C pretend-mitigation-"
"enabled` as well for mitigations that are also target modifiers."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:318
msgid "Defaults"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:320
msgid ""
"We want that the most obvious way to enable mitigations (e.g. `-C stack-"
"protector=strong` or `-C sanitizer=shadow-call-stack`) to turn on "
"enforcement, since that will set people for a safer default where "
"mitigations are enabled throughout."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:325
msgid ""
"However, we do want an easy way for distribution owners (for example, "
"Ubuntu) to turn on mitigations in a non-enforcing way, as is done today e.g. "
"[by Ubuntu with `-fstack-protector-strong`](https://wiki.ubuntu.com/"
"ToolChain/CompilerFlags). Distributions can't easily add a new mitigation in "
"an enforcing way, as that will cause widespread breakage, but they can "
"fairly easily turn a mitigation on in a non-enforcing way."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:331
msgid ""
"We do want the combination of defaults to combine in a nice way - if the "
"distribution sets `-C stack-protector=strong -C allow-partial-"
"mitigations=stack-protector`, and the user adds `-C stack-protector=strong`, "
"we want the result to be stack-protector set to strong and enforcing. This "
"is done by the \"resetting\" behavior of the CLI - setting `-C stack-"
"protector=strong -C allow-partial-mitigations=stack-protector -C stack-"
"protector=strong` acts as if the `-C allow-partial-mitigations=stack-"
"protector` was not passed."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:338
msgid ""
"Distributors and packagers can set defaults for mitigations by setting some "
"sort of `RUSTFLAGS` argument, or by patching the compiler to act as if there "
"was an \"implicit\" prepended `RUSTFLAGS` argument."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:344
msgid "The standard library"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:346
msgid ""
"One big place where it's very easy to end up with mixed mitigations is the "
"standard library. The standard library comes compiled with just a single set "
"of mitigations enabled (as of Rust 1.88: [PIC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Position-independent_code), [NX](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executable-"
"space_protection), [`-z relro -z now`](https://www.redhat.com/en/blog/"
"hardening-elf-binaries-using-relocation-read-only-relro)), and without `-Z "
"build-std`, it is only possible to use the mitigation settings in the "
"shipped standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:352
msgid ""
"If we find out that some mitigations have a positive cost-benefit ratio for "
"the standard library (probably at least [`-Z stack-protector`](https://"
"github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/114903)), we probably want to ship a "
"standard library supporting them by default, but in a way that still allows "
"people to compile code without mitigations (for example, ship a `libstd` "
"with `-C stack-protector=strong`, but allow users to compile their own code "
"with `-C stack-protector=none` using that `libstd`) if that fulfills their "
"security/performance tradeoff better."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:364
msgid "Why not target modifiers?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:366
msgid ""
"The [target modifier](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3716) feature "
"provides a similar goal of preventing mismatches in compiler settings."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:369
msgid "There are several issues with using target modifiers for mitigations:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:371
msgid "The name unsafe-allow-abi-mismatch"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:373
msgid ""
"The name of the flag that allows mixing target modifiers, `-C unsafe-allow-"
"abi-mismatch`, does not make sense for cases that are not \"unsafe ABI "
"mismatches\". It also uses the word \"unsafe\", which we prefer not to use "
"except in cases that can result in actual unsoundness."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:378
msgid "The behavior of unsafe-allow-abi-mismatch"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:380
msgid ""
"The behavior of `-C unsafe-allow-abi-mismatch` is also not ideal for "
"mitigations."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:382
msgid ""
"The flag marks a crate as basically having a \"wildcard target modifier\", "
"which allows it to compile with crates with any value of the target modifier."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:385
msgid ""
"This is quite good for the original use case - it's an \"I know what I am "
"doing\" flag that allows \"runtime\" crates to be mixed in even if they "
"subtly play with the ABI rules - for example, in a kernel, where floating "
"point execution is mostly forbidden, there are a few compilation units using "
"real floats. To call into them, first you call some special functions that "
"make the floating point registers usable, and then you call into the CU "
"safely by not having any floats in the signature of the function on the "
"boundary ([example by Alice Ryhl](https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/"
"channel/131828-t-compiler/topic/Target.20modifiers.20and.20-Cunsafe-allow-"
"abi-mismatch/near/483871803))."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:393
msgid ""
"However, for mitigations, the expected case for disabling mitigations is "
"less people knowing what they are doing, and more people that don't agree "
"with the performance/security tradeoff they bring. In that case, we should "
"allow the executable-writer to be aware of the tradeoff being made, rather "
"than letting libraries in the middle decide it for them."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:399
msgid "Target modifier, enforced mitigation, neither, or both?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:401
msgid "For every single mitigation-like flag:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:403
msgid ""
"If mixing values of the flag can cause unsound behavior, sanitizer false "
"positives, or crashes, it should be a target modifier."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:405
msgid ""
"If mixing values of the flag can hurt a production program's security "
"posture, it should be an enforced mitigation."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:407
msgid ""
"If the flag is a sanitizer intended for use only in fuzzing and testing, and "
"mixing values of it does not lead to unsound behavior, it should be neither."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:410
msgid ""
"If mixing values of the flag hurt a production program's security posture in "
"some cases, and lead to unsound behavior in other cases, it should be both. "
"I am not aware of a current flag that fits this pattern."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:415
msgid "`-emit=component-info`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:417
msgid ""
"One possible \"big\" alternative would be emitting a component info file, "
"via `-emit=component-info`. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:422
msgid "\"components\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:424
msgid "\"type\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:424
msgid "\"crate\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:425
msgid "\"name\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:425
msgid "\"foo\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:426
msgid "\"mitigations\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:426
msgid "\"a\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:432
msgid ""
"We could then have Cargo run over that file and look for missing mitigations."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:434
msgid ""
"Without support in Cargo, this would be not good enough since it would take "
"an annoying enough amount of effort for people to scan the generated json "
"files."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:438
msgid "It is important to avoid this sort of JSON being bikeshed-land."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:440
msgid ""
"An advantage is that people could write their own custom policies, which "
"would take some pressure off bikesheds for things like overflow checks."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:443
msgid ""
"In some sense, this is moving complexity from rustc to Cargo, which I'm not "
"sure reduces it overall."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:446
msgid ""
"A disadvantage is that it would be possible to enable a mitigation in rustc "
"but not in Cargo (e.g. by setting `RUSTFLAGS`), which would make it non-"
"enforced."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:449
msgid ""
"A big disadvantage of this is that it adds much complexity and deeper-than-"
"we-want integration to projects that are _not_ using Cargo, since they have "
"to implement the scanning integration themselves."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:453
msgid ""
"Even if this alternative is not selected, some sort of `-emit=component-"
"info` does feel like a good feature, though one that deserves a separate "
"RFC. There does not seem to be a _conflict_ between `-emit=component-info` "
"and `-C allow-partial-mitigations`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:458
msgid "Why not an external tool?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:460
msgid ""
"This is somewhat hard to do with an external tool, since there is no way of "
"looking at a binary and telling what mitigations its components have."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:464
msgid ""
"There are however some external tools that do check for mitigations, but "
"they have limitations:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:467
msgid ""
"[`hardening-check(1)`](https://manpages.debian.org/testing/devscripts/"
"hardening-check.1.en.html) exists, but its check for stack smashing "
"protection only checks that at least 1 function has stack cookies, rather "
"than checking that every interesting function has it enabled."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:470
msgid ""
"The Linux kernel has [`objtool`](https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/"
"git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/tools/objtool/Documentation/objtool.txt?"
"id=5cd64d4f92683afa691a6b83dcad5adfb2165ed0), which checks for some other "
"mitigations (for example, retpolines). It however needs to access the `.o` "
"object files rather than the final linked executable or shared library - "
"which requires its user to control the linking process - and also has "
"hardcoded limitations that make it only suitable for the Linux kernel, "
"rather than being useful as a general-purpose tool."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:476
msgid ""
"Fedora has a tool called [`annobin`](https://sourceware.org/cgit/annobin), "
"which is able to parse the [`.gnu.build.attributes`](#gnubuildattributes-1) "
"section, which compilers can use to indicate mitigation support. That does "
"fit our needs, but is specific to the GNU/Linux world - it is desirable to "
"have a solution that works on all platforms."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:481
msgid ""
"There is a similar tool called [`checksec`](https://www.trapkit.de/tools/"
"checksec/), with similar limitations."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:488
msgid "Mitigations that are manifestly visible from the program header"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:490
msgid ""
"For some mitigations, the mitigation is enabled for an entire program "
"executable, by setting a flag in the program header."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:493
msgid ""
"Many of these mitigations normally require all the code within the program "
"to be compatible with them, and therefore they normally work in a way where "
"every object file indicates whether it is compatible with them, and with the "
"mitigation being enabled for the ELF if all of the comprising object files "
"are compatible with it."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:499
msgid "Mitigations that act like that:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:501
msgid ""
"Position-independent code ([`ET_DYN`](https://stackoverflow.com/"
"questions/34519521/why-does-gcc-create-a-shared-object-instead-of-an-"
"executable-binary-according-to/34522357#34522357))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:502
msgid ""
"Non-executable stack ([`PT_GNU_STACK`](https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/"
"LSB_5.0.0/LSB-Core-generic/LSB-Core-generic/progheader.html))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:503
msgid "[`.note.gnu.property`](#notegnuproperty) mitigations"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:505
msgid ""
"Other mitigations are also manifest from the program header, but are "
"selected at link-time, without requiring any changes to the compilation of "
"the comprising object files:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:509
msgid ""
"relro ([`PT_GNU_RELRO`](https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/LSB_5.0.0/LSB-"
"Core-generic/LSB-Core-generic/progheader.html))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:511
msgid ""
"In these cases, programs such as [`hardening-check(1)`](https://manpages."
"debian.org/testing/devscripts/hardening-check.1.en.html) can often be used "
"to check the flags in the program header and see whether the mitigation is "
"enabled."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:518
#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:614
msgid ".note.gnu.property"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:520
msgid ""
"The `.note.gnu.property` field contains a number of properties (for example, "
"[`GNU_PROPERTY_AARCH64_FEATURE_1_BTI`](https://docs.rs/object/0.37/object/"
"elf/constant.GNU_PROPERTY_AARCH64_FEATURE_1_BTI.html)) that are used to "
"indicate that the compiled code contains certain mitigations, for example "
"BTI (`-Zbranch-protection=bti`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:525
msgid ""
"When linking multiple objects, the linker sets the resulting property to be "
"the logical AND of the properties of the constituent objects."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:528
msgid ""
"For protections such as BTI, the mitigation can only be turned on if all "
"code within the compiled binary supports it - if one of the object files "
"doesn't, the loader has to leave the mitigation turned off entirely. The ELF "
"loader uses the value of the property within the loaded executable to decide "
"whether to turn on the mitigation."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:534
msgid ""
"If it could be arranged, using `.note.gnu.property` could allow mitigation "
"tracking to propagate across languages - with the final compilation step "
"intentionally erroring out if the property is not enabled. However, this is "
"also a disadvantage - adding a new property to `.note.gnu.property` requires "
"cooperation from the target owners."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:539
msgid ""
"Therefore, it might be useful as a future step with cooperation from the "
"target owners, but is not good if we want to be able to add new enforced "
"mitigations without requiring cooperation from all platforms."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:545
msgid "Passing enforced mitigations to the linker"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:547
msgid ""
"Currently, the mitigation enforcement RFC does not do anything for native "
"dependencies, which can be compiled without mitigations within a program "
"that supports mitigations."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:550
msgid ""
"As far as I can tell, the `rustc` compiler currently does not read the "
"object files of native dependencies, but instead passes to the linker "
"arguments that allow the linker to find and link in the object files."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:554
msgid ""
"If a linker accepts flags that enforce certain mitigations - currently, "
"these are mostly [mitigations manifest from the program header](#mitigations-"
"that-are-manifestly-visible-from-the-program-header) - the compiler should "
"pass these flags to the relevant linker."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:558
msgid ""
"Since that is a breaking change as it breaks compilation with existing C "
"code, it might need to be done over an edition or linker-change boundary."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:561
msgid "Use of the lint mechanism"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:563
msgid ""
"In theory, mitigation enforcement could be a collection of lints, accessible "
"via our standard lint infrastructure, and as such e.g. exposed in Cargo via "
"existing lint configuation."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:566
msgid ""
"The problem with this is that lints are designed to be capped - normally, "
"lints are intended for cases where a probable bug exists, but generally, for "
"code that is not currently under active development, users prefer to use the "
"code with a probable, pre-existing bug as opposed to changing it, and cap "
"the lints (using e.g. `--cap-lints=warn`) while sending the warnings to a "
"place that might be handled eventually."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:572
msgid ""
"Lints have a `force-warn` option that always warns, as well as a `forbid` "
"option that overrides `allow`. We would need a `force-error-but-allow-allow` "
"option that overrides capping lints but does not override `allow`, which "
"would make the lint ordering a lattice as opposed to a total ordering, and "
"be undesirable."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:577
msgid ""
"For mitigations, this is an undesirable state, as you generally do not want "
"to deploy code with pre-existing missing mitigations."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:580
msgid ""
"Also, lints are generally controlled by developers, while mitigations are "
"normally controlled by DevSecOps engineers, and it is reasonable to keep the "
"separation functional."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:583
msgid ".gnu.build.attributes"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:585
msgid ""
"This is a [Fedora feature](https://fedoraproject.org/wiki/Toolchain/"
"Watermark). It actually behaves pretty similarly to how we expect mitigation "
"enforcement to work - a compiler plugin written by Fedora makes C/C++ "
"compilers output a `.gnu.build.attributes` section indicating which "
"mitigations are present or absent. The linker aggregates all of these "
"sections, and `annocheck` can be used to check whether an object file "
"declares an interesting mitigation as missing."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:592
msgid ""
"This is currently a Fedora-ism as far as I can tell. It can be used with non-"
"Fedora linkers (of course, in that case, C code will probably not indicate "
"which mitigations it is missing)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:596
msgid ""
"It might be a good idea to coordinate with Fedora and have rustc emit this "
"metadata, but since it only works on Linux platforms, probably better to not "
"solely rely on it."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:601
msgid "Prior art"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:604
msgid "The panic strategy"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:606
msgid ""
"The Rust compiler already _has_ infrastructure to detect flag mismatches: "
"the flags `-Cpanic` and `-Zpanic-in-drop`. The prebuilt stdlib comes with "
"different pieces depending on which strategy is used, although panic landing "
"flags are not entirely removed when using `-Cpanic=abort`, as only part of "
"the prebuilt stdlib is switched out."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:612
msgid "Target modifiers"
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:616
msgid ""
"The `.note.gnu.property` section discussed previously is an example of C "
"code detecting mismatches of a flag at link time."
msgstr ""

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:619
msgid "Unresolved questions"
msgstr "未解決的問題"

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:622
msgid "Future possibilities"
msgstr "未來的可能性"

#: src/3855-mitigation-enforcement.md:625
msgid ""
"A possible future extension could be to provide a mechanism to enforce "
"mitigations across C code and Rust code. This would be an interesting "
"extension, but it would require cross-language effort that will take a long "
"period of time to finish. Similarly, another possible future extension could "
"be to catch mitigation mismatches when using dynamic linking."
msgstr ""
